      "priority": "medium",
      "details": "Develop a script that: 1) Runs on a daily schedule, 2) Chunks the previous day's NDJSON logs, 3) Uses generateContentStream to create summaries, 4) Writes summaries to daily_summary.md files, 5) Uploads summaries to cold storage using files.upload, 6) Maintains an index of available summaries. Include proper error handling and logging for this background process.",
      "testStrategy": "Test the summarization with sample NDJSON files. Verify the chunking logic works with various log sizes and the summaries are properly formatted and stored.",
      "subtasks": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "title": "Create Daily Log Processing and Chunking Module",
          "description": "Develop a module that reads the previous day's NDJSON logs and chunks them appropriately for summarization",
          "dependencies": [],
          "details": "Implementation details:\n1. Create a new module `logProcessor.js` that exports functions for identifying and reading the previous day's logs\n2. Implement a function `getYesterdayLogs()` that determines the date range for 'yesterday' and locates the corresponding log files\n3. Implement a `chunkLogs(logs)` function that splits large log files into manageable chunks for processing (consider memory constraints)\n4. Add helper functions to parse NDJSON format and extract relevant information for summarization\n5. Include error handling for missing files, corrupted data, and other edge cases\n6. Add logging throughout the process to track execution\n7. Write unit tests to verify log identification, reading, and chunking functionality\n8. Test with sample log data of varying sizes to ensure proper chunking behavior\n\n<info added on 2025-04-28T09:05:51.300Z>\n## Implementation Specifics\n\n### Technical Approach for Log Processing\n- Use Node.js streams API (`fs.createReadStream`) for memory-efficient processing of large log files\n- Implement line-by-line reading with `readline` interface to avoid loading entire files into memory\n- Consider using the `split2` npm package for reliable NDJSON parsing that handles edge cases\n\n### Chunking Strategy\n```typescript\n// Example implementation of chunking with token counting\nasync function chunkLogs(logs: LogEntry[], maxTokens: number = 30000): Promise<LogEntry[][]> {\n  const chunks: LogEntry[][] = [];\n  let currentChunk: LogEntry[] = [];\n  let currentTokenCount = 0;\n  \n  for (const log of logs) {\n    // Estimate tokens for this log entry\n    const logContent = JSON.stringify(log);\n    const tokenCount = await models.countTokens(logContent);\n    \n    // If adding this log would exceed max tokens, start a new chunk\n    if (currentTokenCount + tokenCount > maxTokens && currentChunk.length > 0) {\n      chunks.push(currentChunk);\n      currentChunk = [];\n      currentTokenCount = 0;\n    }\n    \n    // Add log to current chunk\n    currentChunk.push(log);\n    currentTokenCount += tokenCount;\n  }\n  \n  // Add the last chunk if not empty\n  if (currentChunk.length > 0) {\n    chunks.push(currentChunk);\n  }\n  \n  return chunks;\n}\n```\n\n### Date Handling\n- Use `luxon` or `date-fns` for reliable date manipulation across timezones\n- Store logs in UTC format but display in local timezone for readability\n- Include logic to handle logs that span midnight boundaries\n\n### Error Recovery Mechanisms\n- Implement exponential backoff for file read retries\n- Add checkpointing to resume processing after failures\n- Create a recovery log to track partially processed files\n\n### Performance Considerations\n- Benchmark different chunking strategies (by line count, token count, time windows)\n- Consider parallel processing for multiple log files using worker threads\n- Implement caching of recently processed logs to avoid redundant processing\n\n### Testing Strategy\n- Create mock log generators that produce variable-sized entries\n- Test with artificially large entries that exceed chunk size\n- Verify chunking preserves conversation context by keeping related entries together\n</info added on 2025-04-28T09:05:51.300Z>",
          "status": "done",
          "parentTaskId": 5
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "title": "Implement Summary Generation with generateContentStream",
          "description": "Create a module that processes log chunks and generates summaries using the generateContentStream API",
          "dependencies": [
            1
          ],
          "details": "Implementation details:\n1. Create a new module `summaryGenerator.js` that handles the summarization process\n2. Implement a function `generateSummary(logChunks)` that takes chunked logs from the previous subtask\n3. For each chunk, call the generateContentStream API with appropriate prompts to create meaningful summaries\n4. Design the prompt template to extract key information from logs (e.g., important events, patterns, anomalies)\n5. Combine chunk summaries into a cohesive daily summary with sections and formatting\n6. Format the final summary as markdown with appropriate headers, lists, and sections\n7. Include error handling for API failures, malformed responses, and timeout scenarios\n8. Implement retry logic for failed API calls with exponential backoff\n9. Add detailed logging of the summarization process\n10. Write tests using mock API responses to verify summary generation functionality\n\n<info added on 2025-04-28T09:12:27.051Z>\n## Implementation Plan for Subtask 5.2: Implement Summary Generation with generateContentStream\n\n### Technical Implementation Details\n\n#### Prompt Engineering\n```typescript\nfunction createPrompt(chunk: LogEntry[], date: string): string {\n  return `\nYou are analyzing system logs for ${date}. Summarize the following log entries, focusing on:\n1. Critical errors and exceptions\n2. Performance bottlenecks (response times > 500ms)\n3. Unusual access patterns or security concerns\n4. System state changes (restarts, config updates)\n\nFormat your response in markdown with appropriate headers and bullet points.\n\nLOG ENTRIES:\n${JSON.stringify(chunk, null, 2)}\n`;\n}\n```\n\n#### Stream Handling Implementation\n```typescript\nasync function handleContentStream(stream: AsyncIterable<GenerateContentResponse>): Promise<string> {\n  let summaryText = '';\n  \n  for await (const response of stream) {\n    if (response.candidates?.[0]?.content?.parts?.[0]?.text) {\n      summaryText += response.candidates[0].content.parts[0].text;\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return summaryText;\n}\n```\n\n#### Retry Logic Example\n```typescript\nasync function callWithRetry<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>, maxRetries = 3, initialDelay = 1000): Promise<T> {\n  let retries = 0;\n  while (true) {\n    try {\n      return await fn();\n    } catch (error) {\n      if (retries >= maxRetries) throw error;\n      \n      const delay = initialDelay * Math.pow(2, retries);\n      console.log(`API call failed, retrying in ${delay}ms...`, error);\n      await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, delay));\n      retries++;\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n#### Combining Chunk Summaries\n```typescript\nfunction combineChunkSummaries(summaries: string[], date: string): string {\n  return `# System Log Summary for ${date}\n\n## Overview\n${generateOverviewSection(summaries)}\n\n${summaries.map((summary, i) => `## Log Segment ${i+1}\\n${summary}`).join('\\n\\n')}\n\n## Recommendations\n${generateRecommendations(summaries)}\n`;\n}\n```\n\n#### Performance Considerations\n- Use batch processing for large log sets (>1000 entries)\n- Implement concurrency control with Promise.all and chunking:\n  ```typescript\n  const summaryPromises = chunks.map(chunk => \n    callWithRetry(() => summarizeChunk(chunk, date))\n  );\n  const chunkSummaries = await Promise.all(summaryPromises);\n  ```\n- Consider adding a cache for similar log patterns to reduce API calls\n\n#### Testing Strategy\n- Create mock log data representing various scenarios (errors, normal operation, etc.)\n- Test retry mechanism by injecting temporary failures\n- Validate markdown formatting with regex patterns\n- Measure performance with different chunk sizes to determine optimal configuration\n</info added on 2025-04-28T09:12:27.051Z>",
